#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903ab6ef-1061-5e3e-b364-8d1f3612e2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 0$ and $t = \\frac{π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080736} {:answer/id #uuid "b719540a-3d1b-541c-a25f-37f85c560c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080737} {:answer/id #uuid "09b038a3-f3ff-5256-91cf-4136c486c90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = 0$ and $t = π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080738} {:answer/id #uuid "0b89156f-4ae0-56a5-a5cd-892305079937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080739} {:answer/id #uuid "57d9904a-2762-5743-b8cb-547235bb12dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = π$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080740}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a36e582-b023-505c-8f4b-5b6d26a24def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080735}] :question/id #uuid "57cb918d-01c6-5793-92dd-c253fd54156c" :question/math-academy-id "q-21080" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\cos 2t + 5, y = 5\\sin 2t + 4, 0 \\le t < π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0deb773d-8fd3-5717-8d81-b036329021ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288130} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9ee4d4-6360-5ed2-836d-031b9471868c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288317} {:answer/id #uuid "cebe0140-f0cc-5ccb-ac3d-d23a3bad85d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289412} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e4345a-11f1-5c37-a93b-12a41d947a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289450} {:answer/id #uuid "e4544b18-b948-5075-9873-247a5ee72340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4544b18-b948-5075-9873-247a5ee72340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289547} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad14c4bb-1d4a-5d58-9bcb-70314b824013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad8de0d6-0fb0-5332-a3cd-f5bf44efff31" :question/math-academy-id "q-21207" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38170285-5909-5a87-b81e-7614b331e561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254153} {:answer/id #uuid "61b95ad1-c677-5a1d-9693-7fe23f0898f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254545} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3251-ef46-57f3-8565-b37dbcb4bbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f808726-09d3-5692-a435-5070f152cddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494} :answer-field/id #uuid "7461ea8a-3c02-5867-96b2-21f3b9f03a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e5d7b42-493e-5694-92f6-e6ea1c2f15fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122946" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d597a98-c055-5f99-9bee-b68fbbdd90cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253785} {:answer/id #uuid "0f02ed52-8673-5cc6-908c-e93ec89237c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253801} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc3b08-7caf-5e27-b46d-574424299686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254482} {:answer/id #uuid "80913870-a077-54f8-a853-f63a26091309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254807} {:answer/id #uuid "e09500e3-b088-5b78-9a17-f963b264a9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80913870-a077-54f8-a853-f63a26091309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254807} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5601a63-8c5d-5677-8eb5-dd5f3c21d7f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "259b7265-587e-5b30-9b76-d6e0205b048e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122947" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3bc6ba-dba0-565a-9d3e-6d25c11cdc38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d9076ce-d69d-5ee0-95b8-de3533c91e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-350403" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8a6d76-f112-5663-9241-788ae415e493" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb60be2-41b3-5c75-b56a-529c11e5a236" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0473c92-6bbb-51d0-9d33-3793374dc209" :question/math-academy-id "q-350414" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a85035df-3f18-5f4a-8b7d-4120d3a258fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393516} {:answer/id #uuid "8814ca04-45d2-5482-92fe-bcab232b18b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393517} {:answer/id #uuid "57d3f469-2ccf-551a-b2e0-ffe31d3bca63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393518} {:answer/id #uuid "6712e860-a397-555b-b691-57588df29d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393519} {:answer/id #uuid "332bdb7a-e350-5dc0-a978-64f0aa49c697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8814ca04-45d2-5482-92fe-bcab232b18b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393517} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e4af98-e57b-515a-916f-95959841d208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393515}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5eafad9-4c2d-5469-bbd9-3cd0fcca968a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75278" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087462 :question/id #uuid "021d25c6-08db-5f20-a89b-110389d08e29" :question/math-academy-id "e-3224" :question/problem "What is the equation of the circle given below?\n\n![](images/79/79111226781bc9d3ea9c00e217b93b9661d1e70bdfbee688c555dd899cf3d80e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-1,1)$ and the radius of the circle is $r = 3.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d6988b-3220-578f-ba1c-cd86b9e3dbf9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048811 :topic/math-academy-id 842 :topic/title "Equations of Circles Centered at the Origin"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820b9446-f775-5a58-99f1-aaf9f71a8103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)^{2} + (y - 3)^{2} = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085885} {:answer/id #uuid "c318a1b1-16d8-524f-aee8-c1ea71c3ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)^{2} + (y - 3)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085886} {:answer/id #uuid "068143ae-abb3-5246-a247-cf645ce4106c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1)^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085887} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed9340b-9c0a-5a97-8f6d-ce4434dc381d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 1)^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085888} {:answer/id #uuid "60740bd2-2976-58f3-a163-82475cedea7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 1)^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085889}] :answer-field/id #uuid "eca3cee0-5ebd-5882-ad43-8b8aee761777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085884}] :question/id #uuid "cbbe18ba-d440-571c-88bf-2fea5eb3f37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54187" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbd35be4b79c007c5587a4e07e38aa2380d0a7c6461dcbaaa52671aff07e9309.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a571fa5-6dc8-5ca2-992e-277d87b9678f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085903} {:answer/id #uuid "9264f737-f2ce-5294-afaa-d5fecdb6088c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085904} {:answer/id #uuid "d579fc13-bb11-5148-bda0-0651f708228d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085905} {:answer/id #uuid "d64b7122-6221-546a-9bad-9f6771db9127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085906} {:answer/id #uuid "032bf460-dd3b-5c90-89c1-04eb08632abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085907}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d070a38b-bc69-519e-9a20-5b9484c4ef9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085902}] :question/id #uuid "e11496b7-2fc3-5c53-8069-22f2e85621c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-54210" :question/problem "![](images/94/941552af685a46484581668a04525b2eaf863db242f6e797dcda8b2c3cbf4ca3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea6a32f-49ef-57a9-b429-d346d779f130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ea6a32f-49ef-57a9-b429-d346d779f130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234691} :answer-field/id #uuid "455de079-3c57-5a2c-b063-2ae6c0d12dd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70fc8621-7b34-535c-a0b8-10d27db72e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-167932" :question/problem "Write the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(3,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f753d8c-60a5-57f8-bdf7-1dfca23922c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250687} {:answer/id #uuid "194d005a-074c-5528-8e5a-5c077edd8e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250774} {:answer/id #uuid "315ac89d-372a-5365-ae8a-8dbdf9c4b3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250971} {:answer/id #uuid "85cecc42-57d3-50a8-a690-6891b14e42dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251673} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e225b3-c9f2-51bf-8ede-3befd132f224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315ac89d-372a-5365-ae8a-8dbdf9c4b3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250971} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf481095-8ecd-5669-92d1-9a4c71b18f2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1378faa1-06f3-5893-a7e8-8251ca750847" :question/math-academy-id "q-116929" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b69a99c42bcdbd7e6ae3434862019b792da6bea498fd92a36595722d4fb66995.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf2e972-6b80-5e52-afb7-0e4c5944055c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250935} {:answer/id #uuid "c20eabe8-bd89-54f4-9981-d68d1fd1e853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252265} {:answer/id #uuid "cfda17b2-0685-57a1-b791-0077608dabf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252387} 